2.initial_plan:
Model the movement as repeated multiplication by x modulo m. Since gcd(x,m)=1, multiplication by x permutes residues modulo m, so the graph decomposes into disjoint directed cycles. To guarantee catching the mouse from any unknown start, we need at least one trap in every cycle, and one trap per cycle is sufficient. Therefore the answer is the number of cycles of the permutation f(i)=i*x mod m.

Count cycles without iterating up to m. Group numbers by d=gcd(i,m). For i=d*j with gcd(j,m/d)=1, multiplication by x keeps gcd with m equal to d, so each group is invariant. Inside the group with n=m/d, states correspond to units modulo n, and the map is multiplication by x on U(n). Every orbit there has length ord_n(x), so the number of cycles contributed by this group is phi(n)/ord_n(x). Summing over all divisors n of m gives:
answer = sum_{n|m} phi(n) / ord_n(x), with n=1 contributing 1.

So we need:
1) factorize m;
2) enumerate all divisors n of m;
3) for each n, compute phi(n);
4) compute multiplicative order ord_n(x mod n) using factorization of phi(n): start with ord=phi(n), and for each prime p dividing ord, divide out p while x^(ord/p) ≡ 1 (mod n).

Because m<=1e14, trial division up to sqrt(m) is sufficient in Python for this range if implemented carefully enough for contest-style input with one test case.

